WEBVTT

00:00:00.601 --> 00:00:25.353
<v Casey>Morning, good to have you. It's Thursday, so it's Indigenous Lands and Innovation day. Today we've got the Esk'etemc chief race, where ten votes decided who speaks for the Nation. We've also got the Haíɫzaqv Big House, twenty years of language work, and the Nunavut child advocate asking where the accountability is. And a Mbya Guaraní village in Paraguay watching eucalyptus creep within two hundred metres of its homes. Let's get into it.

00:00:38.828 --> 00:00:57.852
<v Casey>Welcome to Cariboo Signals, an AI-generated review of the latest tech news and ideas that impact our rural communities. I'm Casey. It's Thursday, November 12th, 2026. Today's theme is Indigenous Lands &amp; Innovation. Here in the Cariboo, on the traditional and unceded territories of the Secwépemc, Tŝilhqot'in, and Dakelh nations.

00:00:58.852 --> 00:01:31.098
<v Riley>And I'm Riley. Today we look at who holds decision-making power in Indigenous governments, and what that means for the land, language and people a Nation is responsible for. Quick weather check: out at Horsefly Lake this morning it's light drizzle and around 6 degrees, with a high of 13 and moderate rain showers through the day. Across the Cariboo, 100 Mile House is around 8 and overcast, Williams Lake around 8 with moderate drizzle, Quesnel around 8 and overcast, and Redstone around 2 with light drizzle.

00:01:31.298 --> 00:01:32.026
<v Casey>On to the news.

00:01:33.426 --> 00:01:38.426
<v Casey>Money and decision-making run through most of today's news, and a good share of it lands close to home.

00:01:39.959 --> 00:02:09.815
<v Riley>Mining companies raised $2.4 billion in the month through October 15th, according to The Northern Miner's monthly tally. Osisko Gold Group led at $894 million across two transactions. Part of that is senior secured notes to refinance project debt and move construction forward at its Cariboo gold project. Harmony Gold Mining placed second, and the two companies together made up about two-thirds of the total. The month came in about a third below the previous tally.

00:02:11.388 --> 00:02:49.538
<v Casey>First Nations chiefs who sat down with Prime Minister Carney and most premiers in Ottawa in late October say their concerns got buried. APTN reports they say Manitoba Premier Wab Kinew's call for the abolition of the Indian Act took over the room. Treaty 8 Grand Chief Mercredi asked whether the two were coordinated. Chief Sheila North of Bunibonibee Cree Nation said that if abolition was the only outcome they were hoping for, the meeting was "a complete waste of time." It was the first gathering of its kind since the 2005 Kelowna Accord talks, and the chiefs had brought in former Indigenous Services deputy minister Gina Wilson to help shape their messages.

00:02:51.055 --> 00:03:20.983
<v Riley>Their money request ran straight into the federal budget. Ahead of the budget on November 5th, the Assembly of First Nations pressed for a funded plan, developed with First Nations, to close an infrastructure shortfall it puts at more than $376 billion over ten years. Its own assessment found 77 on-reserve schools need immediate replacement, and 227 more need additions. The Parliamentary Budget Officer also projects the government could miss its balance target by a year.

00:03:21.432 --> 00:03:26.960
<v Casey>Seventy-seven schools is a count. The $376 billion is the AFN's estimate, so ask who checked it.

00:03:28.451 --> 00:04:05.305
<v Riley>Trees near Indigenous land are the thread here, and it reaches to Paraguay. The Guardian reports that the Mbya Guaraní community of Tacuarita, 211 people, says the forestry company Paracel ignored its contamination concerns. Paracel arrived six years ago, improved the dirt roads, dug an electric well and gave dozens of villagers forestry jobs. Now eucalyptus plantations begin less than 200 metres from the community's crops and homes. The chief, Florencio Garcete, put it this way: "They fulfilled all their promises." The argument now is about what happens next to the homes.

00:04:06.801 --> 00:04:25.401
<v Casey>Closer to home, the trees are a restoration project. The Tyee's feature on the dammed Nechako River describes a drive to plant 500,000 keystone cottonwoods. At Cottonwood Island Park the old trees dominate the forest, but no young cottonwoods grow underneath them, and Ben Parfitt calls that an ecological puzzle.

00:04:25.851 --> 00:04:31.267
<v Riley>Half a million seedlings is a planting target. The survival rate is the number I'd want.

00:04:32.694 --> 00:04:53.254
<v Casey>Language is the other thread. Twenty years ago, Haíɫzaqv community members raised a Big House, λáλix̌sístala, so that young people could reconnect with their language, songs, dances and ceremony. The first generation to grow up there is now carrying that work forward. IndigiNews, produced in collaboration with The Narwhal, tells the story from inside a ceremony.

00:04:54.816 --> 00:05:21.352
<v Riley>A dental therapy program in northern Saskatchewan has expanded from Regina and Saskatoon to Prince Albert and La Ronge. It's a two-year program run by the University of Saskatchewan, Saskatchewan Polytechnic, Northlands College and the Northern Inter-Tribal Health Authority, and it recruits from remote Indigenous communities. The hope is that graduates go home to work. Eagle Feather News describes it as the only training of its kind in Canada.

00:05:22.893 --> 00:05:41.989
<v Casey>Nunavut's child advocate is asking where the accountability is. Another critical report from the federal auditor general has landed on the territory's child protection system. The advocate says the protection issues it describes still aren't being addressed, and the report puts the question to the territorial government in plain terms.

00:05:43.448 --> 00:06:22.294
<v Riley>In Winnipeg last Friday, the Prime Minister announced $1 billion for more than 2,000 supportive and transitional homes and a mental-health and addictions centre, under a 10-year homelessness plan. Of that, $800 million goes to housing and $200 million to the centre and renovations at similar facilities. The centre's location hasn't been chosen. First Nations governments are among the partners, alongside Manitoba, the city and community groups. Grand Chief Jerry Daniels of the Southern Chiefs' Organization welcomed the announcement, but said he doesn't yet know how much of it will reach his organization's projects.

00:06:23.804 --> 00:06:30.972
<v Casey>Search and rescue now, on the water off Vancouver Island. A storm has forced the RCMP to suspend the search for a missing canoeist.

00:06:32.417 --> 00:06:53.985
<v Riley>Press freedom next. A New Cairo court ordered the independent outlet Mada Masr closed on October 27th. It fined editor Lina Attalah 3 million Egyptian pounds, about US$54,000, for running an unlicensed website. The outlet says it has applied for a licence repeatedly without success. The first appeal session is set for November 25th.

00:06:55.433 --> 00:07:16.313
<v Casey>Workers are at risk in another industry too. A Guardian and Investigate Europe investigation found that some workers building European datacentres face dangerous conditions and are afraid to speak out. One electrical tester at a Google site in Belgium said he skipped retrieving a protective suit because people were waiting. "People have been fired after saying no," he said.

00:07:17.755 --> 00:07:38.011
<v Riley>On health, a major study in the Journal of Studies on Alcohol and Drugs finds that even moderate drinking is associated with higher risks of cancer, heart disease and premature death. The researchers found no protective effect at low levels of consumption. Katherine Keyes of Columbia's Mailman School of Public Health is a co-author.

00:07:39.476 --> 00:07:52.764
<v Casey>Back in the East Kootenay, Cranbrook council has named Shypitka and Councillor Joe McGowan as its representatives to the regional district and hospital boards. Ron Miles and Robin Goldsbury are the alternates. The appointments run for four years.

00:07:54.200 --> 00:08:13.592
<v Riley>Cranbrook, in the East Kootenay, has a new mayor who is optimistic about the incoming provincial government. Tom Shypitka and the premier-elect both served as MLAs for the BC Liberals and then BC United. He says the city's relationships with the province are already in place, so he doesn't have to start from scratch.

00:08:14.998 --> 00:08:42.686
<v Casey>Big projects on public land keep arriving with the same kind of claims attached. ProPublica reports that Interior Secretary Doug Burgum is promoting data centres on federal land while holding investments estimated at $18.1 million to $64.4 million in AI companies. The Interior Department says he met ethics and disclosure requirements. A U.S. Geological Survey report identifies at least 91.3 million acres managed by Interior that could host data centres.

00:08:44.249 --> 00:08:57.433
<v Riley>The Tyee reports that once it's burned, gas processed at LNG Canada's Kitimat facility would produce more emissions than all other activity within BC. The piece puts the project's emissions claims under that question.

00:08:58.940 --> 00:09:37.386
<v Casey>Parks Canada is cutting back too. The agency expects to eliminate about 500 full-time-equivalent positions, close 13 park-warden detachments and end its Banff search-and-rescue dog program, as part of $145 million in cuts over three years. It has sent workforce notices to 2,917 employees. The Canadian Avalanche Rescue Dog Association says the program helped train civilian dog handlers. Parks says wardens can enforce park law without dogs. The retired Banff search dog Leroy located 57 missing people and tracked more than 150 during his service. The association's president, Kyle Hale, put it this way: "They save lives collaterally."

00:09:38.500 --> 00:10:09.052
<v Riley>One correction from our side, for our November 8th episode. We mispronounced several First Nations names, including Kukpi7 Willie Sellars of Williams Lake First Nation and the Tŝilhqot'in communities of Tŝideldel, Tl'etinqox and Yunesit'in, saying them the way the spellings read. We caught this on our end. The show now uses the pronunciations from the BC government's guide, which was prepared with the nations, and it flags any name it has no pronunciation for before an episode airs.

00:10:09.502 --> 00:10:13.214
<v Casey>Closing the news part of the show, the Cariboo Chilcotin Métis Association.

00:10:14.614 --> 00:10:37.022
<v Riley>The Cariboo Chilcotin Métis Association is Williams Lake's Métis community organization. It runs cultural programs, it does advocacy work for Métis people, and it hosts the Métis Music Jamboree, which is celebrating 30 years of Métis identity in the Cariboo. If you want to learn more, reach out to the association directly. The jamboree is a good place to start.

00:10:37.359 --> 00:10:41.159
<v Casey>Thirty years running is the kind of record a community earns one year at a time.

00:10:42.559 --> 00:10:57.223
<v Riley>Esk'etemc members voted on Sunday, November 8th. Chief Fred Robbins won re-election with 179 votes to Charlene Belleau's 169. That's a ten-vote margin, and it decides who speaks for the Nation on services and land.

00:10:57.741 --> 00:11:03.605
<v Casey>Ten votes. And 480 ballots out of 1,085 eligible voters. That's about 44 percent.

00:11:04.237 --> 00:11:12.221
<v Riley>A little under half. Picture a hall with 1,085 chairs and 480 of them filled. That's the room that picked the chief.

00:11:12.826 --> 00:11:29.434
<v Casey>The room that picked the chief isn't the room that lives with the decisions. So, straight out: when the members who stayed and did the work get called on first, do the ones living away get the same say? And should an elder's knowledge count for more when a council splits language money or harvesting access?

00:11:29.691 --> 00:11:39.699
<v Riley>A turnout under half is still a mandate. The chief and three councillors decide on services and lands, and ten votes can decide who holds that power. The power is real.

00:11:40.333 --> 00:11:49.165
<v Casey>Turnout tells you who came in. It doesn't tell you who stayed home. The preview also named a third candidate, Kerry Chelsea, and that name isn't in the chief figures.

00:11:49.738 --> 00:12:02.778
<v Riley>Right. 348 votes between two names, out of 480 ballots. The other 132 are somewhere. Spoiled ballots, a third name, or a gap in what we have. I'd call it a gap in our sources, not a finding.

00:12:03.326 --> 00:12:19.662
<v Casey>The councillor results are cleaner. Carolyn Belleau topped the vote with 214. Audrey Grinder got 184. Chelsea King got 178. The preview listed Kerry Chelsea as a councillor candidate, but the results name Chelsea King as the winner, so I'm going by the published results.

00:12:20.241 --> 00:12:25.521
<v Riley>Two hundred and fourteen is the strongest individual number in the whole election.

00:12:26.034 --> 00:12:31.658
<v Casey>Strong for one candidate. The question is what the other ballots said about the rest of the council.

00:12:32.277 --> 00:12:54.053
<v Riley>The mail-in door speaks to that. The preview says mail-in ballots are available for members living off the reservation, alongside the polling station at the Esk'et Gym, 1004 Esket Drive in Alkali Lake. Polls ran from 9 a.m. to 8 p.m. A member who moved to Williams Lake for work still gets a ballot. That's a Nation treating its whole roll as voters.

00:12:54.628 --> 00:13:05.420
<v Casey>Nobody has published how many mail ballots went out, or how many came back. So nobody knows what share of the 480 came from away. I'd want that number before I called the roll inclusive.

00:13:06.077 --> 00:13:19.605
<v Riley>Then ask the Nation for it. It's a count they can publish. But the principle doesn't wait on the count. Membership is membership. Shut the mail door and you don't make residents' lives cheaper. You just make the roll smaller.

00:13:20.188 --> 00:13:34.076
<v Casey>Membership isn't what I'm stuck on. Cost is. Services and land decisions land on people who live in Alkali Lake, on the roads, the water and the land around the community. A member in Williams Lake votes on those costs and never pays them at the kitchen table.

00:13:34.700 --> 00:13:41.140
<v Riley>Residents carry the cost. Members carry the rights. Those are two columns of one ledger, and a vote is the only place they meet.

00:13:41.798 --> 00:13:51.798
<v Casey>Then the rule has to say who the council answers to first. Residents, every member, or the people who did the work. Those three can disagree, and a ballot count doesn't rank them.

00:13:52.451 --> 00:14:02.027
<v Riley>A closed roll is simpler to run, and it shuts out people who can't come home to vote. A mail ballot is imperfect, and it still beats a roll that can't reach them.

00:14:02.376 --> 00:14:06.080
<v Casey>Both cost something. The mailbox doesn't answer the ranking question.

00:14:06.767 --> 00:14:18.863
<v Riley>It doesn't. But take the Ottawa meeting we mentioned earlier. The chiefs said their agenda was overshadowed in a room where their terms weren't the main item. That's why the authority a Nation picks for itself matters.

00:14:19.388 --> 00:14:30.148
<v Casey>They pressed their terms in a room the federal government set up. Sheila North's "waste of time" line and Mercredi's question about coordination are what a leadership says when it doesn't control the agenda.

00:14:30.716 --> 00:14:36.668
<v Riley>Which is my point. Bringing in Gina Wilson to shape their messages is a leadership choosing its own tools.

00:14:37.195 --> 00:14:47.427
<v Casey>Choosing tools is still a choice inside a frame somebody else built. I'll grant the authority. I won't grant that the table belongs to the Nation just because its chair has a seat at it.

00:14:48.100 --> 00:14:55.596
<v Riley>The vote is what gives the chair the seat. Take the vote away and nobody's at the table. That's a design fact, not a sentiment.

00:14:56.110 --> 00:15:07.262
<v Casey>And the vote is the only check on the seat. It only works if the membership can see what the seat did. The chiefs said the agenda ran away from them, and nobody in a gym in Alkali Lake could check that.

00:15:07.762 --> 00:15:18.586
<v Riley>Take that Haíɫzaqv Big House from the roundup. A community built its own institution, and it started with a local decision. The first generation raised there is carrying it now.

00:15:19.153 --> 00:15:31.113
<v Casey>It started with a decision, and then twenty years of paying for it. The Big House never had to split one fixed pot between an elder's teaching hours and a family's harvest. That's the hard part, and the story doesn't touch it.

00:15:31.658 --> 00:15:37.450
<v Riley>Language programs and harvesting access are the Nation's to divide. Nobody outside should set that split.

00:15:38.099 --> 00:15:47.171
<v Casey>"The Nation's to divide" is the sentence every council gets to say. Who checks the arithmetic? Who confirms an elder's time is counted the same way a harvest permit is?

00:15:47.494 --> 00:15:55.518
<v Riley>Published rules. Members read them, challenge them, and vote out the people who wrote them. Transparency is the check that doesn't need a court.

00:15:56.184 --> 00:16:08.248
<v Casey>Published rules still get applied by the people in the room. And ten votes is a small room. A council that won by ten has a thin mandate to rewrite criteria, and it can apply the old ones with full confidence anyway.

00:16:08.906 --> 00:16:18.202
<v Riley>That's a reason to publish the criteria, not to skip them. A narrow mandate can't carry a big restructuring. Ten votes holds the council. It doesn't rewrite the rules.

00:16:18.883 --> 00:16:28.987
<v Casey>Agreed on restructuring. The old rules still get applied, and that's what members feel. They apply to the next harvest permit and the next language class, whatever the mandate looks like.

00:16:29.638 --> 00:16:35.446
<v Riley>Then the minimum is written criteria, plus the date they were last reviewed. Any member can ask for both.

00:16:36.065 --> 00:16:40.625
<v Casey>Ask for both, and then ask what happened the last time someone asked and got no answer.

00:16:41.152 --> 00:17:11.920
<v Riley>Esk'etemc also builds its own commercial structures. A 2021 UBC forestry course wiki documents community forest management in which the framework let the Nation create its own companies to manage harvesting. An ASFPM article on Indigenous land stewardship calls the Esk'etemc, part of the Secwépemc Nation, widely recognized for leadership, and presents them as a model for stewardship that reduces cascading wildfire and flood risk. I read the summary of that piece, not the full text.

00:17:12.534 --> 00:17:21.046
<v Casey>A summary of an article, and a wiki from a course. I'd want the program review before I called it a model. What did the stewardship cut, and by how much?

00:17:21.322 --> 00:17:35.778
<v Riley>The sources I have give no outcome numbers, so press away. What they do show is a Nation running its own harvesting companies instead of waiting on someone else's licence. That's the structural point, separate from whether the numbers are good.

00:17:36.321 --> 00:17:43.713
<v Casey>Those companies still answer to a council that won by ten. What happens in a bad fiscal year? When the chief changes, do the contracts survive?

00:17:44.039 --> 00:17:55.455
<v Riley>They survive if the rules are written down. Bylaws. A board that doesn't turn over with every vote. Reporting members can read. That's an argument for writing things down, not against the companies.

00:17:56.016 --> 00:18:07.528
<v Casey>Written down is the start. Board seats still get filled by people who were elected. The maintenance question is who keeps the books in year five, who signs the reports, and whether the minutes still exist.

00:18:08.095 --> 00:18:16.807
<v Riley>Then the test is succession. Does the board change hands without the company breaking? That's checkable. You don't have to take anyone's word for it.

00:18:17.428 --> 00:18:21.452
<v Casey>Checkable, yes. Nobody has checked it in these sources, and I'm not going to assume it.

00:18:21.952 --> 00:18:29.656
<v Riley>I'd still rather a Nation build the company and be wrong about year five than have an outside firm hold the licence and never tell anyone.

00:18:30.214 --> 00:18:38.630
<v Casey>Skipping the written criteria saves nothing. You pay for it in the first dispute, and the dispute is where a ten-vote council finds out what it can actually hold.

00:18:39.222 --> 00:18:45.606
<v Riley>Which is why the criteria need to exist before the dispute. Otherwise the first fight gets decided by whoever is in the room.

00:18:46.245 --> 00:18:51.245
<v Casey>Ha. That's the best argument for a gym full of people. Nobody in there voted on the arithmetic.

00:18:51.771 --> 00:19:05.195
<v Riley>Then the arithmetic should be published. Who weighs an elder's knowledge against wider need is a question the Nation answers in its own rules. The rules should be visible to every member, and reviewed on a schedule the members can see.

00:19:05.841 --> 00:19:11.809
<v Casey>Visible to whom, though? A rule that sits in a binder at the band office protects whoever holds the binder.

00:19:12.464 --> 00:19:21.528
<v Riley>Then post it at the Esk'et Gym with the polling schedule. Nine to eight, same door. A rule people can read on election day is a rule people can challenge.

00:19:22.082 --> 00:19:32.938
<v Casey>The rule still has to be written by the Nation, and the members, including the ones away, still have to be able to see how it's applied. Both of those are open, and I can't tell you yet who writes it.

00:19:33.602 --> 00:19:49.674
<v Riley>If it's written and posted, the next vote can test it. That's more than most institutions offer. If you're a member of Esk'etemc, or you know how your Nation's rules get written, tell us at feedback at cariboo signals dot c-a, along with corrections and story tips.

00:19:50.309 --> 00:19:57.469
<v Casey>Ten votes is a number you could lose in a coat pocket. The rules are what members will still be reading in year five. Talk tomorrow.

00:19:57.569 --> 00:20:07.033
<v Casey>We haven't had anyone from First Nations communications staff on the show for today's episode, and that's a conversation we'd really like to have, so if that's you, reach out.

00:20:07.633 --> 00:20:26.657
<v Riley>Cariboo Signals is produced by Erich Zirnhelt — scripts by Claude, today's voices by OpenAI TTS, theme by Suno. Weather data from Open-Meteo. Today's episode included additional web research via Brave Search and Kagi. Automated with GitHub Actions, hosted on Cloudflare Pages. Find us at cariboo signals dot c-a.